</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Three error types reveal distinctive model-specific deficiencies:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Missing required parameter.</span>
MiniMax-M2 (13.4 per instance at difficulty-20) and GPT-5 (8.7 at difficulty-10) exhibit dramatically high rates, suggesting systematic failure to enumerate the complete parameter set as tool schemas grow complex.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wrong format.</span>
GPT-5 uniquely suffers from this error across difficulty 5–15 while all other models produce near-zero counts, indicating a systematic format adherence failure for unfamiliar APIs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wrong node type.</span>
DeepSeek-Reasoner and DeepSeek-Chat show the highest rates at difficulty-20, reflecting a failure to correctly identify which nodes are executable tools.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The distinct error profiles confirm that different models exhibit qualitatively different failure signatures, suggesting that targeted improvements could yield disproportionate gains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Efficiency Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond correctness, we examine the <em id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">average number of tool calls</em> consumed per instance as a measure of problem-solving efficiency.
Fewer tool calls indicate that the model navigates the dependency graph with less redundancy—fewer incorrect tool invocations and tighter execution plans.
Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Efficiency Analysis ‣ 4 Fine-Grained Trajectory Analysis ‣ AgentEscapeBench: Evaluating Out-of-Domain Tool-Grounded Reasoning in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports the mean tool-call count for each model–difficulty pair.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:186.3pt;">
        
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="S4.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average tool calls per instance</span> across difficulty levels.
Lower is better.
<span id="S4.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> = fewest in column; <span id="S4.T3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underline</span> = second fewest.
<sup id="S4.T3.9" class="ltx_sup">⋆</sup>Min. Required: the average minimum number of tool calls needed to solve an instance (theoretical lower bound).
</figcaption>
</figure>
        
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
